# This unit's real hardware configuration — the Raspberry Pi 5 XLeRobot.
#
#   set -a && source deploy/pi/xlerobot.env.example && set +a
#   python3 sidecar/xlerobot_sidecar.py        # then open http://<pi>:8900/control
#
# The Pi sibling of deploy/mac/xlerobot.env.example (lex-robot#174 §1). Most
# values transfer; the ones that do NOT are serial device names, camera indices
# and the camera resolution — each measured on THIS host and noted as such
# below. Where a value is inherited from the Mac file unchanged, it says so.

LEX_ROBOT_HW=1
LEX_ROBOT_SIDECAR_PORT=8900

# ── arms ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# /dev/ttyACM* numbering is ASSIGNMENT-ORDERED and can swap between boots,
# which on this robot means the arms silently trading places along with the
# tower and wheels that share their buses. /dev/serial/by-id/ encodes the
# adapter's own serial number instead and is stable across replug and reboot.
#
# Verified on 2026-08-26 by reading Min/Max_Position_Limit out of each arm's
# servo EEPROM and matching it against the calibration files: 6/6 joints match
# xle_left on 5B3D043715 and 6/6 match xle_right on 5B61033220. Do not infer
# this from which side of the robot an arm appears to be on — the arms have
# been physically swapped once already and the adapter pairing did not change.
LEX_XLE_LEFT_PORT=/dev/serial/by-id/usb-1a86_USB_Single_Serial_5B3D043715-if00
LEX_XLE_RIGHT_PORT=/dev/serial/by-id/usb-1a86_USB_Single_Serial_5B61033220-if00

# Must match the calibration files in
# ~/.cache/huggingface/lerobot/calibration/robots/so_follower/<id>.json.
# Both are already present on this host, recovered from motor EEPROM rather
# than re-run — do NOT run lerobot-calibrate (lex-robot#174 §3, #151).
LEX_XLE_LEFT_ID=xle_left
LEX_XLE_RIGHT_ID=xle_right

# Caps each commanded step. Keep it: a second line of defence under whatever
# envelope the caller enforces. (Inherited from the Mac unit unchanged.)
LEX_XLE_MAX_REL_TARGET=8

# ── cameras ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Measured on this host, 2026-08-26. These are NOT the Mac's indices — that
# unit has head=0, left=2, right=1. Do not infer them from enumeration order.
#
#   head  — established by nudging the pan/tilt tower and watching which view
#           moved. Only video0 responds; the other two are static.
#   wrists— established by moving each gripper 217 ticks and diffing frames.
#           The left gripper moves video4 3.6x more than video2; the right
#           gripper moves video2 7.3x more than video4. Each confirms the other.
LEX_XLE_CAMERA_HEAD_INDEX=0
LEX_XLE_CAMERA_LEFT_INDEX=4
LEX_XLE_CAMERA_RIGHT_INDEX=2

# NOT the Mac's reason. On the Mac this is a hard requirement: that topology
# cannot carry three cameras at 1080p and the third silently returns no frame
# (#162). This host CAN — all three stream 1920x1080 MJPG at ~29 fps
# concurrently, verified on the delivered pixels rather than on the property
# readback (lex-robot#174 §2).
#
# 640x480 is kept here for a different reason: the RECORDER, not the bus. The
# teach loop (sync_read + capture + cvtColor + imwrite q85, two cameras) costs
# a median 11.6 ms/frame at 640x480, 23.4 ms at 1280x720 and 49.5 ms at
# 1920x1080 — against a 50 ms budget at 20 Hz, where a third of frames miss.
# 1280x720 is the safe upgrade if more resolution is wanted; 1080p is not, and
# would also write ~15 GB/hour to the card on an overnight run.
LEX_XLE_CAMERA_WIDTH=640
LEX_XLE_CAMERA_HEIGHT=480

# This unit calibrates the gripper the OPPOSITE way to the sidecar's default:
# 0 is CLOSED and 100 is OPEN. A property of the robot, not of the host, so it
# is inherited from the Mac file unchanged. Without these, release_arm drives
# the gripper further shut while reporting success.
LEX_XLE_GRIPPER_OPEN_PCT=100
LEX_XLE_GRIPPER_CLOSED_PCT=0

# ── kinematics ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# placo resolves on aarch64 Linux — installed and importing from the venv on
# this host, no build from source needed (lex-robot#174 §4). Sparse-clone the
# URDF per SIDECAR.md; kept out of /tmp so it survives a reboot.
LEX_XLE_URDF_PATH=${HOME}/.local/share/so-arm100/Simulation/SO101/so101_new_calib.urdf
LEX_XLE_URDF_TARGET_FRAME=gripper_frame_link

# ── base ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The wheels share the RIGHT arm's physical bus rather than having their own
# port (lex-robot#145). Confirmed on this host: adapter 5B61033220 answers ids
# 1-6 plus 9 and 10, while 5B3D043715 answers 1-6 plus the tower's 7 and 8.
# UNVERIFIED: which physical wheel is ID 9 and which is 10 was never
# established. If a forward command turns the robot, swap these two.
LEX_XLE_BASE=diff
LEX_XLE_BASE_SHARED_ARM=right
LEX_XLE_BASE_LEFT_ID=9
LEX_XLE_BASE_RIGHT_ID=10

# ── not set, and why ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# LEX_XLE_GRANT_PATH   defaults to manifests/xlerobot.capsule.json
# LEX_XLE_VISION_URL   set to the GPU box running sidecar/vision_service.py —
#                      that half stays off the Pi (deploy/VISION_SPLIT.md).
#
# Linux note: none of macOS's TCC camera gating exists here. Membership of the
# `dialout` group is the only permission concern (XLEROBOT_SETUP.md §3.2).
#
# The pan/tilt tower (servos 7, 8) sits on the LEFT arm's bus and carries the
# head camera. It ships with torque OFF and it does drift: measured moving 174
# ticks (~15 deg) unattended over a single evening. Hold it before calibrating
# a CameraModel, and check it afterwards with
# `python sidecar/bus_preflight.py --tower-calib <camera_calib.json>`.
